                <pb facs="0362" n="254" sameAs="frus:frus1927v02/pg254"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">393.11/435: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to
                        the Minister in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >MacMurray</hi>)</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1927-01-31T20:00:00-05:00"><hi rendition="#italic">January 31,
                                1927—8 p.m.</hi></date></dateline></opener>
                <p>38. Your telegram 76 of January 25, 4 p.m.<anchor n="4" xml:id="d243-f1"/> You
                    should inform consuls concerned that the authorization granted to them to incur
                    expenses actually necessary in the evacuation of American citizens covers only
                    additional expenditures such as demurrage for vessels which may be essential in
                    making transportation facilities available <pb facs="0363" n="255"
                        xml:id="pg255"/> to endangered American citizens and does not include
                    ordinary charges for transportation such as the usual steamship fares. If,
                    however, any American citizen is unable to meet such ordinary charges they may
                    be defrayed for him on his written undertaking to refund the amounts as soon as
                    practicable. Inasmuch as funds for this purpose are limited, Department must be
                    promptly informed of all expenditures authorized.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Kellogg</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
